1
00:00:00,179 --> 00:00:00,179


2
00:00:05,178 --> 00:00:07,742
Long before the Christian era,

3
00:00:07,867 --> 00:00:10,758
along the shores beside the sea,

4
00:00:10,507 --> 00:00:15,182
there lived pagan peoples,
 with art within their hands.

5
00:00:19,128 --> 00:00:24,130
They built systems
 to understand the patterns,

6
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:30,766
with intricate designs
 on vases now shattered.

7
00:00:31,093 --> 00:00:36,346
This was the age of the ancient Greeks. 
 Alexandria would become the center of civilization.

8
00:00:36,472 --> 00:00:44,666
Within its library, 
 culture would reach beyond the gods.

9
00:00:55,172 --> 00:01:01,305
Upon the silver ashes of Alexander, the patron,

10
00:01:01,406 --> 00:01:10,530
Ptolemy built a new tapestry for the East.

11
00:01:10,631 --> 00:01:16,387
And military might, 
 with its soldiers and generals,

12
00:01:16,512 --> 00:01:23,148
gave way to the scientists 
 gathered in Alexandria.

13
00:01:24,782 --> 00:01:38,229
This was the age of the ancient Greeks...

14
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:07,009
In right-angled triangles,

15
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:12,263
the square on the side opposite the right angle

16
00:02:13,017 --> 00:02:27,947
is equal to the sum of the squares on the sides that form the right angle.

17
00:02:24,453 --> 00:02:27,947
A young student has questioned my proof.

18
00:02:28,148 --> 00:02:34,231
He constructed it himself with a straightedge and tablet,

19
00:02:34,332 --> 00:02:39,962
but seeing it is not enough— that is what his mind tells him.

20
00:02:40,138 --> 00:02:45,064
He says it has not been proven upon an established foundation,

21
00:02:45,265 --> 00:02:50,192
with rigorous boundaries and underlying axioms.

22
00:02:50,443 --> 00:02:55,797
By questioning Thales and Hippocrates, Euclid

23
00:02:55,923 --> 00:03:01,000
shakes a structure that has endured for centuries.

24
00:03:01,930 --> 00:03:06,430
He said in the agora: “The theorem of Pythagoras has not been proven.”

25
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:17,288
Perhaps I am too old to understand… perhaps the Academy is not enough. He cannot stay. I will let him leave.

26
00:03:17,992 --> 00:03:29,655
In right-angled triangles, the square on the side opposite the right angle...

27
00:04:13,089 --> 00:04:19,951
I will leave and abandon

28
00:04:45,363 --> 00:04:56,297
the skeptical companions who care for me, and my teacher, who carefully holds back

29
00:04:56,397 --> 00:05:15,500
words of affection for me, though I seem heartless. I have made my decision: I must leave. For Alexandria.

30
00:05:15,022 --> 00:05:28,495
In Cyrene, an unfinished study awaits me, but travelling there by land would be far too dangerous.

31
00:05:28,621 --> 00:05:41,741
A distant colony—yet perhaps there I may find the traces left behind by Eudoxus.

32
00:05:55,088 --> 00:06:08,863
From Megara, the route can be considered safe. Travelling by sea, frightening though it may be

33
00:06:08,988 --> 00:06:23,366
would be more realistic than crossing the desert. There is a distant port I must reach: Alexandria.

34
00:06:35,858 --> 00:06:50,059
The Etesian winds have swept the sky clear, the air is light, and I can breathe. The climate is different from that of the mainland: the scent of sea, stone and papyrus, in Alexandria.

35
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:38,118
Demetrius of Phalerum has introduced me to a young mathematician.

36
00:07:38,118 --> 00:07:45,483
He says there is no need for gods or sacred numbers.  Only definitions.

37
00:07:45,383 --> 00:07:53,250
Perhaps he is a simpleton, or perhaps he will sweep away the mysticism of the Pythagoreans.

38
00:07:52,948 --> 00:08:02,877
He shall be admitted to the Library.  I will leave the judgment to History.

39
00:08:08,834 --> 00:08:16,601
A point is that which has no part.

40
00:08:16,802 --> 00:08:25,851
A line is breadthless length.  The ends of a line are points

41
00:08:45,029 --> 00:08:54,907
From the premises, knowledge descends to earth, as was to be demonstrated!

42
00:09:07,726 --> 00:09:11,270
I do not claim that I am right; I ask only that you grant me this:

43
00:09:11,547 --> 00:09:13,558
let a line be drawn between two given points,

44
00:09:13,734 --> 00:09:17,026
and, by the same concession, let those points be moved apart.

45
00:09:23,009 --> 00:09:25,095
If you now accept that I may do this,

46
00:09:25,195 --> 00:09:27,634
and with the same grace you are willing to imitate me,

47
00:09:27,734 --> 00:09:29,669
then there is no need to comprehend infinity:

48
00:09:29,896 --> 00:09:32,937
it will be defined within language.

49
00:09:33,113 --> 00:09:46,259
There is a third kind: space,  the receptacle of all becoming,  itself formless.

50
00:09:52,995 --> 00:09:57,871
What has been constructed is a chain of links,

51
00:09:57,972 --> 00:10:02,396
joining fields of knowledge and holding them together.

52
00:10:02,496 --> 00:10:09,484
Things equal to the same thing are also equal to one another.

53
00:10:13,430 --> 00:10:21,599
From the premises, knowledge descends to earth, as was to be demonstrated!

54
00:11:04,556 --> 00:11:09,231
And there is only one risk, one danger to avoid:

55
00:11:09,357 --> 00:11:15,892
if the first link touches the last, closing the chain into a collar.

56
00:12:20,867 --> 00:12:30,242
Only you remain, and you know it. You have no place among the others.

57
00:12:30,393 --> 00:12:41,855
You were never brought in; you were never needed.

58
00:12:43,036 --> 00:12:56,006
POSTULATES four  POSTULATES I do not need  POSTULATES another  POSTULATES

59
00:13:11,716 --> 00:13:13,903
I have already proved twenty-eight.

60
00:13:14,003 --> 00:13:17,246
I will succeed without your help,

61
00:13:17,346 --> 00:13:23,655
just as I have succeeded before, without adding another one.

62
00:13:28,959 --> 00:13:40,270
POSTULATES four  POSTULATES I do not need  POSTULATES another  POSTULATES

63
00:13:50,550 --> 00:13:56,382
If I wish to prove upon these pages

64
00:13:56,507 --> 00:14:02,213
that the interior angles are congruent,

65
00:14:02,314 --> 00:14:06,888
I will have to bow before the Muses

66
00:14:07,039 --> 00:14:10,432
and beg them for one more.

67
00:14:13,775 --> 00:14:24,533
POSTULATES four  POSTULATES I do not need  POSTULATES another  POSTULATES

68
00:14:51,881 --> 00:15:11,964
My lord, Ptolemy,  I am grateful to you for welcoming me here

69
00:15:12,944 --> 00:15:18,449
Thirteen volumes of theorems and axioms

70
00:15:18,625 --> 00:15:24,330
I have already completed, I have already demonstrated.

71
00:15:24,431 --> 00:15:30,011
But what you ask of me, I cannot do,  because:

72
00:15:30,313 --> 00:15:42,503
There is no royal road to geometry

73
00:15:42,704 --> 00:15:56,730
(Ptolomey I) I created Alexander’s city. I laid the foundations of Alexandria.

74
00:15:56,906 --> 00:16:09,926
I welcomed you into the Library.  You studied from those books that every ship arriving here  was politely compelled to surrender to me.

75
00:16:25,359 --> 00:16:35,916
Like an ungrateful son,  the man I welcomed answers me curtly:

76
00:16:36,142 --> 00:16:48,660
There is no royal road to geometry.

77
00:17:03,716 --> 00:17:16,937
(Hypatia) Tell me about Greece, about your geometry.  In which library is the path preserved?

78
00:17:17,214 --> 00:17:30,184
(Proclus): In Alexandria of Egypt, the Muses guarded everything,  from the most arcane knowledge to the most difficult mysteries.

79
00:17:30,410 --> 00:17:42,324
The great Library will never return.  It was lost in the flames of a brutal and blind war.

80
00:17:42,425 --> 00:17:55,168
But who unleashed this senseless fury,  tearing apart the old fabric to impose a new rite?

81
00:18:09,847 --> 00:18:25,482
Theon preserved Euclid’s Elements,

82
00:18:25,658 --> 00:18:30,835
which are kept here, ready for new challenges.

83
00:18:31,011 --> 00:18:41,468
Upon five postulates and twenty-three definitions,  he proved his theorems using only nine axioms.

84
00:18:43,881 --> 00:18:49,888
Here in Athens, a new position will arise:

85
00:18:50,039 --> 00:18:55,393
demonstration will become the foundation of science.

86
00:18:55,594 --> 00:19:01,425
“Ipse dixit” will remain only as a reminder

87
00:19:01,601 --> 00:19:11,530
that the school now needs a secular body of knowledge.

88
00:19:28,320 --> 00:19:41,064
Today I leave this earth and entrust my work.

89
00:19:41,567 --> 00:19:47,498
Sown like a seed, it will rise from new soil.

90
00:19:47,599 --> 00:19:53,757
My lines, my segments, my circles and their centers,

91
00:19:54,210 --> 00:20:00,468
will be born again in May, but in a new language.

92
00:20:00,594 --> 00:20:12,357
They will become encrypted codes, encapsulated within form,  and they will offer new answers to questions not yet asked.

93
00:20:15,801 --> 00:20:25,051
What I did not dare to do will be done by someone not yet born,

94
00:20:25,554 --> 00:20:32,466
within a new creed, surpassing the method of exhaustion.

95
00:20:32,591 --> 00:20:39,453
And through essential calculus, without ruler or compass,

96
00:20:39,629 --> 00:20:48,955
He will speak of infinities without seeming mad any longer.

97
00:21:40,960 --> 00:21:45,836
Here in Alexandria, the fire of my life goes out,

98
00:21:47,671 --> 00:21:53,402
and now the Muses will decide whether it was worthwhile.

99
00:21:53,528 --> 00:22:01,043
They will leave an epitaph, an inscription of my own:

100
00:22:01,144 --> 00:22:13,988
Euclid lies within this earth, he who studied geometry.
